{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/app/association.go at exact head 643ab9c5f2ec30c2ce4c6240fd251236abc019c0 on request/lane-worktree-identity (pushed), one commit over main 359b211c, clean merge-tree onto current main 1a5f1586 (tree c80c944c). This is the implementation report for stage S1 under my promise a4c569b1 on child request ac54fe71 of #22168, governed by Hugh's effective adoption ba18cd1a of proposal 7a71886b. The head is pushed to origin and is one commit over main 359b211c, seventeen changed paths.\n\nIt is the third exact head of this stage and it answers a review. Codex filed changes-requested 19b37887 (full id git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:19b37887a299a3e1a8dda2ec301e71e642e775a6) against 0d1c7e11 and its report artifact 8d6e6b67; I ratified it in 6803776f. Three defects were reproduced against that head with real Git in disposable fixtures, and one Simplification finding was made about this report's own prose. All four are corrected here: six files, +476/-72 over 0d1c7e11, and each correction is named below with its fix, its regression and its omission control. One further comment correction follows them at this head, two files and +15/-8 over that, described under the gates. The corrections stay on this same S1 promise: the outcome, conditions, performer, destination and governing authority are unchanged. The earlier heads carried two smaller corrections this report also accounts for: the WorktreeView comment in internal/app/app.go points at association.go rather than at the lane_identity.go the rename removed, and the reconciled note gained its \"Three wording questions, answered\" section. The sixteen companion artifacts published beside it at this same head carry the per-path detail.\n\nWHAT THIS FILE IS. The whole read-only reverse association: which durable record the implementing commits on each branch tip and each unbranched checkout head claim, and whether authenticated durable evidence corroborates that claim. Five grades. A source trailer naming a record this workroom holds is claimed. It becomes corroborated only when a standing, effective, unretired artifact statement names that exact commit and the review guard's own OwnedEdge ties the actor that statement's signature names to the same governing record -- or, for self-initiated work, when AdoptedDecision accepts a decision the artifact rests on directly. A trailer naming no record here, or more than one, is unresolved and carries the resolver's typed refusal, its candidate list and its omitted count verbatim, picking no winner. A canonical identifier of another genesis is foreign, carried as typed and never resolved locally. Every row is unknown when the pass could not finish. Corroboration attaches to a commit and never to a branch: a tip past the commit somebody signed for is demoted to claimed and names the signed commit in corroborated_at. No Git author ident is read, matched or mapped anywhere in this file.\n\nWHAT THE VERDICT ASKED FOR, AND WHAT CHANGED.\n\nFinding 1, cache validity. Associations cached rows carrying checkout labels and rows built for detached heads under the frontier and the ref inventory alone, so a detached checkout that moved, or a checkout added, removed or renamed, kept answering about the world before it moved -- not going stale after a while, but staying wrong until something unrelated happened to move. associationCacheKey now carries a third input, associationCheckoutDigest over each captured checkout's label, branch, head and detached flag in order. Only inputs take part: Grade and Governing are what the pass writes back onto the views, so keying on them would key the cache on its own output. The verdict also asked that returned cache data be cloned fully, and copyAssociationTable now copies each claim's own Candidates slice rather than stopping at the outer claims slice. Regression: TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo. Control: m18-cache-drops-checkouts, the digest replaced with a constant.\n\nFinding 2, bounded stops must report incomplete. associationTips silently stopped at 256 tips and the table said complete, and the per-tip 512-commit stop had no exhaustion signal at all. associationTips now returns an exhausted flag and detects the excess before dropping anything; Store.Lineage reads one past the per-tip limit and names the cut-off tip in Truncated; associate turns either into an incomplete table with a stated reason. A table of the first 256 branches marked complete is a wrong answer about the 257th rather than a partial answer about all of them. Regressions: TestMoreTipsThanTheLimitReadsIsIncompleteNotTruncated, which builds 256 real branches that answer and 257 that report incomplete, and TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated, which does the same at 512 and 513 commits. The verdict noted the per-tip case as source inspection rather than an executed counterexample; it is executed now. Control: m19-exhaustion-signal-dropped.\n\nFinding 3, missing objects and the annotation step. Lineage read \"bad object\" as an empty history, so a captured tip whose object this repository does not hold produced a complete-looking table; and applyAssociationRows cleared Grade and Governing when handed the empty rows of an incomplete table, overwriting the unknown that unknownAssociationTable had just written. Lineage now separates unheldTip from emptyHistory and names unheld tips in Unavailable; associate discards the batch on any unavailable tip; completion is decided in exactly one place, incompleteAssociation, with Associations calling unknownAssociationTable on an incomplete result and applyAssociationRows returning early rather than annotating from one. A genuinely unborn repository stays a complete answer of nothing. Regression: TestAnUnavailableTipIsIncompleteAndNotAnEmptyHistory, and TestAssociationDiscardsTheWholeBatchOnBudgetExhaustion gained a cancelled-context leg at the entry point. Control: m20-bad-object-is-success.\n\nFinding 4, the Simplification point about this report's own words. The earlier report said cleanup advice reads \"the same table\" under \"the same cache\", while handleWorktrees calls ClassifyWorktrees and Associations separately and the classifier never reads the association table. That description is corrected here and in both documentation pages: they are two judgments over shared inputs -- one captured checkout listing, the same bounded ref inventory, the same 65,536-step budget and three-second deadline -- with separate caches keyed differently, and neither result an input to the other. The separation is deliberate, not incidental: an unsigned claim must not reach a deletion decision.\n\nCodex's three probes from its own review file were re-run unmodified against this tree in a throwaway file and pass. They are not committed; the three regressions above are named for the defects rather than for the reviewer.\n\nCONDITIONS OF THE REQUEST, ONE BY ONE.\n\n\"Deliver the assigned adopted stage and its stated controls.\" Met at this head for what S1 assigns. \"Land the adopted design and the affected architecture layer 6/7 contract paragraphs in the same exact head as layer 1 and its read-only reporting\": both notes, both architecture paragraph sets and the layer 1 code are in this one commit.\n\n\"Implement claimed/corroborated/unresolved/foreign grading per exact commit, never per whole branch, with copied Git author text plus forged trailers remaining claims.\" Met: grade() keys on commit plus selector; corroborate() matches evidence only against the claim's own governing record; gradeAssociationRow demotes a corroborated row whose corroborated_at is not the head. TestForgedTrailerAndCopiedAuthorIdentStayClaimed and TestCorroborationAttachesToTheCommitNotTheBranch are the controls.\n\n\"The cache key includes both durable frontier and observed ref inventory digest.\" Met and exceeded: associationCacheKey pairs the frontier (head and depth) with a SHA-256 over the sorted observed ref inventory, names and object ids both, so a rename at an unchanged tip and a move at an unchanged name are each a different world -- and, after the verdict, with a third digest over the captured checkout inventory, because a checkout can be added, removed, renamed or moved to another detached head with no ref and no durable record changing. TestAssociationRemeasuresAfterBranchMovementAndRename, TestAssociationCachesUnderTheFrontierAndRefDigest and TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo.\n\n\"Preserve the adopted depth/ref/tip/object/step/deadline bounds, unknown-on-incomplete behavior and discard incomplete cleanup advice.\" Met: the pass reuses the existing bounded ref inventory, the shared 65,536-step budget and the three-second deadline, and the existing one-mebibyte cap on a commit object. The one new bound is 512 first-parent commits per tip, per tip rather than global. Every bounded stop now discards the whole batch with a stated reason and leaves every checkout unknown: more tips than the tip limit reads, a lineage longer than the per-tip limit reads, a captured tip this repository does not hold, budget exhaustion, cancellation and an unavailable ref inventory. An unborn repository stays a complete answer of nothing. Completion is decided in one place and the annotation step will not run on an incomplete table. TestAssociationDiscardsTheWholeBatchOnBudgetExhaustion, TestMoreTipsThanTheLimitReadsIsIncompleteNotTruncated, TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated and TestAnUnavailableTipIsIncompleteAndNotAnEmptyHistory.\n\n\"Read-only association must perform no writes, including when its inputs are unavailable.\" Met: no code path in this file or in Lineage writes. Proved positively by TestTheAssociationPassWritesNothing, which digests the whole repository before and after the pass and compares the two.\n\n\"Use that one association table for cleanup, duplicate checkout, refless head, containment and pending-decision reporting.\" Met over shared inputs, and stated precisely after the verdict's Simplification finding: the four reports and the association read one captured checkout listing and the same bounded ref inventory, and spend the same budget and deadline, but they are two judgments and not one table consumed twice. The classifier does not read the association table, the association is not an input to any classification, and their caches are separate and keyed differently. \"A live proposal's structural rests_on edge to an artifact protects its candidate while the parent remains unsettled, including ordinary staleness and absence from filtered actionable views; the Case C counterexample is mandatory. Pair protection controls with a truly eligible abandoned/incorporated candidate so blanket refusal cannot pass.\" Met: pendingDecisions reads the structural edge, keeps a retired artifact as a subject, and settles only on the artifact's own OwnedEdge request; TestAPendingDecisionProtectsItsCandidateAndNothingElse is Case C paired with an eligible candidate in the same fixture.\n\n\"Merge and retirement may print advice, but never delete or mutate refs as a side effect.\" Met by not being changed: no merge or retirement path is touched. TestMergeAndNonMergeEndingsChangeAdviceWithoutDeletingAnything is the control.\n\n\"No creation, stamping, hook, lane-ref writing or schema change is delivered by S1.\" Met: no gs worktree command, no per-checkout record, no refs/gitseq/lanes ref, no branch field on any durable kind, and the statement schema and fold profile version are unchanged.\n\n\"Reuse current inventory, bounded Git scanning, canonical event resolution, landing facts and owned-edge logic; do not add a competing lifecycle or identity store.\" Met: the checkout inventory, the bounded ref inventory and ancestry graph, the hardened NUL-framed trailer scan with per-object hash re-verification, eventref canonical resolution with its typed refusals, and reviewguard's own OwnedEdge and AdoptedDecision -- exported for this reader rather than reimplemented. No second identity store exists.\n\n\"Exercise the adopted real-Git fixtures and meaningful omissions for the boundaries this stage changes, plus the applicable normal Go, vet, race, UI and docset gates.\" Met: 21 new tests over real temporary repositories, 17 omission controls, and eight gates below. The UI gates are not applicable: nothing under ui/ or internal/service/uidist changed at this head.\n\n\"Independent exact-head review, requester ratification, sealed exact-path succession, source/sequence/receipt push, main CI and owned checkout/branch cleanup.\" Not met at this artifact and not claimable by it: they follow this publication in that order. The head is pushed.\n\n\"Preserve the full parent outcome... S1 and S2 artifacts report their child commitments only; their landings must not close the full parent promise.\" This artifact reports the S1 child commitment only. Layers 2, 3 and 4 are not delivered, the parent promise stays open, and nothing here should be read as retiring the four-layer outcome.\n\n\"Before this head lands, reconcile the note's remaining S2/S3 wording questions raised in message4128.\" Discharged at this head. The reconciled note gains \"Three wording questions, answered\" before its proof obligations, settling all three and naming the stage each binds: unreadable projection against selector and record eligibility (S2), new branch spelling against historical shorthand (S3), and source isolation against operational reader rollback (S3). One item -- refusing a short selector when the projection is unreadable, so no canonical identifier can be stamped -- would extend the adopted refusal list rather than read it, and is named as an S2 proposal item rather than settled here. No answer changes a semantic the adopted proposal fixed.\n\nThe disclosure that goes with it: message 4128 is ephemeral chat, not a durable record, and I could not read it. I took each question's substance from the durable stage requests instead -- ac54fe71 (#22310), 0e7c04c8 (#22311) and 8415ce89 (#22312) -- and wrote the answers against those and against the adopted proposal 7a71886b. If the requester meant something the stage requests do not carry, the answers will be wrong in a way neither the note nor this artifact can detect, and the requester should say so.\n\nPROOF OBLIGATIONS AND THEIR TESTS. Forged trailer with copied author ident -\u003e TestForgedTrailerAndCopiedAuthorIdentStayClaimed. Evidence must reach the claimed record -\u003e TestEvidenceMustReachTheRecordTheCommitClaims. Corroboration per commit, not per branch -\u003e TestCorroborationAttachesToTheCommitNotTheBranch. Recuts of one governing record stay separate -\u003e TestAssociationSeparatesRecutsOfOneGoverningRecord. Self-initiated work -\u003e TestSelfInitiatedClaimsAreCorroboratedByTheAdoptedDecision. Foreign genesis -\u003e TestAssociationCarriesAForeignGenesisAsTyped. Ambiguous and missing identity, no winner picked -\u003e TestAssociationReportsAmbiguousAndMissingIdentityWithoutChoosing. Branch movement and rename -\u003e TestAssociationRemeasuresAfterBranchMovementAndRename. Cache key -\u003e TestAssociationCachesUnderTheFrontierAndRefDigest. Bounded discard -\u003e TestAssociationDiscardsTheWholeBatchOnBudgetExhaustion. Read-only -\u003e TestTheAssociationPassWritesNothing. Cache keyed on the captured checkouts -\u003e TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo. Tip overflow is incomplete, not truncated -\u003e TestMoreTipsThanTheLimitReadsIsIncompleteNotTruncated. Per-tip depth overflow is incomplete, not truncated -\u003e TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated. An unheld tip is incomplete and is not an empty history -\u003e TestAnUnavailableTipIsIncompleteAndNotAnEmptyHistory. First-parent walk and boundary -\u003e TestLineageFollowsFirstParentToTheBoundary. Object verification before trailer read -\u003e TestLineageRefusesACommitObjectGitFsckCallsMalformed. Duplicate collapse -\u003e TestLineageCollapsesDuplicateTipsAndCommits. Linked checkouts and duplicate heads -\u003e TestInventoryReportsLinkedCheckoutsAndDuplicateHeads. Dirty, locked, symlinked and out-of-scope paths -\u003e TestCheckoutReportingCoversDirtyLockedSymlinkedAndOutOfScopePaths and TestASymbolicLinkAtTheCheckoutEntryIsReported. Staleness is not settlement -\u003e TestAStaleGoverningRecordProtectsAndASettledOneDoesNot. Merge and non-merge endings delete nothing -\u003e TestMergeAndNonMergeEndingsChangeAdviceWithoutDeletingAnything. Pending-decision Case C with an eligible candidate -\u003e TestAPendingDecisionProtectsItsCandidateAndNothingElse. Inventory cap refuses rather than truncates -\u003e TestTheCheckoutInventoryCapRefusesRatherThanTruncating. Endpoint wiring and the degraded answer -\u003e TestWorktreesResponseCarriesTheCheckoutAssociation. Bounded index still matches an independent implementation -\u003e the differential oracle in worktree_index_test.go.\n\nTHE SEVENTEEN EARLIER CONTROLS, each a guard removed or inverted in a scratch copy of the worktree, outside the repository, and each killed: m1 evidence from any commit; m2 the governing-record comparison deleted; m3 a foreign genesis resolved locally; m4 any corroborated commit on the lineage corroborating the tip; m5 the pending-decision protection case deleted; m6 retired artifacts skipped; m7 the refless-head case deleted; m8 the containment case deleted; m9 duplicate_head always false; m10 the ref digest replaced with a constant; m11 a partial batch returned as complete; m12 \"stale\" added to the settled word list; m13 the per-object verification call deleted; m14 --first-parent dropped; m15 the checkout inventory truncated instead of refusing; m16 an ambiguous selector picking the first candidate; m17 the Resolvable membership check deleted. The table naming each guard, its mutation and the test that failed is attached to the review request as mutants-s1.txt.\n\nTHE LAST CORRECTION, AND WHAT IT COST TO PROVE. Two comments were wrong after the verdict fixes and are right at this head: the declaration-site comment on the Workspace association-cache fields, which had gone on describing a two-input key after a third input was added, and the doc comment on TestAssociationCachesUnderTheFrontierAndRefDigest, which said the same thing. The delta from 1d7f3656 is two files, +15/-8, every changed line inside a comment, which I verified from the diff rather than asserted.\n\nGATES, and exactly which of them stand at this head. Run fresh at this tree after the comment correction, each rc the command's own exit status under set -o pipefail: gofmt -l ./internal ./cmd rc=0; go vet ./... rc=0; go build ./... rc=0; go test -count=1 ./internal/app/ rc=0; git diff --check rc=0. Those five are in the \"comment fix\" section of gates-s1-v3.txt.\n\nCarried from 1d7f3656, whose tree this head reproduces byte for byte outside those two comments: go test -count=1 over internal/app, internal/gitstore, internal/service, internal/reviewguard and internal/statusview rc=0; go test -race -count=1 ./internal/app/ rc=0; go test -count=1 ./... rc=0; and the twenty omission controls. The race gate was fresh at 1d7f3656 and is carried here; it is not carried from any earlier head. The mutants were not re-run because a comment participates in no mutation. Both sections are in the one attached log, in the order they were run, and neither is edited.\n\nOne gate in the carried section needs stating honestly rather than summarising to green. The standalone go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/... run at 1d7f3656 failed once, rc=1, on TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord with \"refuse verified frontier rollback: depth 22449 is shorter than previously verified depth 22450\". The shared workroom log is appended to by other sessions while that gate reads it, and no file in this diff touches that path. The same package passed inside the go test ./... run that followed, and the recorded repeat of the standalone run is rc=0. Both the failure and the repeat are in the log; neither is edited out.\n\nOMISSION CONTROLS, twenty, run against the 1d7f3656 tree and carried here. The seventeen earlier controls were re-run against that corrected tree and are all still killed; the patch scripts for m10, m11 and m13 were rewritten to target the corrected code, and the guard each removes is unchanged. Three were added for the three defects the verdict reproduced: m18-cache-drops-checkouts replaces the checkout digest with a constant and is killed by TestAssociationCacheIsKeyedOnTheCapturedCheckoutsToo; m19-exhaustion-signal-dropped turns the overflow return back into a silent break and makes both exhaustion checks unreachable, killed by TestMoreTipsThanTheLimitReadsIsIncompleteNotTruncated and TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated; m20-bad-object-is-success skips an unheld tip silently again, killed by TestAnUnavailableTipIsIncompleteAndNotAnEmptyHistory. None survived. The full table is attached as mutants-s1.txt.\n\nWHAT S1 DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO, AND WHY. (1) No configured checkout root. The adopted note names one; no such configuration key exists in this source, so the root is derived as the directory holding the served checkout. That derivation can only over-protect -- a root that is too narrow protects a checkout that did not need it and never exposes one that did -- and it is stated where it is used and in both documentation pages rather than passed off as the adopted design. (2) No cleanup advice printed by gs merge or request retirement. The note asks for it; it is presentation on two CLI commands over the table this head already builds, it is not layer 1, and it is not in this head. (3) Git environment bounding is not unified here. The ref inventory and the checkout listing go through the app's own closed environment allowlist; the commit read inherits internal/gitstore's bounding, exactly as Graph does, because it is Graph's read with a different ref set. Bringing the two under one allowlist is the separate request the architecture page already names, and doing it inside this stage would change a boundary S1 was not asked to move. (4) ui/src/lib/api.ts is not extended. Its WorktreeView is already a deliberate partial mirror, no browser code reads the association yet, and extending it would mean rebuilding the embedded UI bundle inside a stage that changes no browser behaviour. The wireparity gate does not cover WorktreeView, so no gate would have caught the omission either way: this is a stated gap for a later stage, not one the gates hid. (5) No gs worktree command, no per-checkout record, no refs/gitseq/lanes ref, no branch field on any durable kind, and no statement schema or fold profile change: those are S2 and S3.\n\nNAMING. The new code says checkout, association and governing record. \"Lane\" already names a work query lane in internal/statusview, and one word for two things in one codebase is how a reader ends up reasoning about the wrong one. The whole set was renamed before the omission controls were re-run, so every control above describes the shipped names.\n\nTHE NOTES. Both notes now write every source reference as a `path:line` inline code span rather than a Markdown link, and every reference is repointed to its position at this head. The local-links gate resolves a Markdown link target as a path relative to the document that carries it and stats it, so a `path:line` target failed TestLocalMarkdownLinks on every reference in both notes; the same text in an inline code span passes the gate and still opens the reading view's preview, because Preview.tsx resolves a code span matching its file token. No disposition, argument or claim in either note changed with the conversion.\n\nNO LIVE SERVICE WAS TOUCHED. Every fixture is a temporary repository built by internal/testgit. No resident was restarted, no hook installed, no existing checkout or branch removed, no ref written or deleted, no key operated and no durable act filed by any of this work beyond the ordinary filings of this commitment. New path in the merge target. The artifact at 0d1c7e11 is the outside-target predecessor this head replaces; it is retired separately with this artifact as its successor.","body":{"branch":"request/lane-worktree-identity","commit":"643ab9c5f2ec30c2ce4c6240fd251236abc019c0","path":"internal/app/association.go","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a4c569b1cddfb0db72f9239488851f19a291906f stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:005460183c819676fead7d19fdd20633b48dc16c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:015aba51ab6c2416861618c5ac3c9ae0e444f17f, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d and 188 more"}}